%==========================================================================
\section{Remaining Challenges and Plan}
\label{sec:plan}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Batches of sequences.} This is the regime where GH200's link stops hiding transfers and speculation's advantage should reappear. DFlash's loop handles one sequence, so we will time single verification steps on batches with synthetic KV, using acceptance traces from real runs.
\item \textbf{Real GH200 numbers.} Rent GH200 time (e.g., Lambda) to check the projected curves. Its CPU is ARM, so we will budget time for setup.
\item \textbf{Deeper prefetch.} With two staging buffers, fetches can run only two layers ahead, which limits hiding at small offloaded fractions. We will sweep the number of buffers and compare against a Pie-style rule that offloads only what transfers can hide.
\item \textbf{Context versus task.} Separate the effect of context length on acceptance from that of task type, and measure how many guesses to verify: verifying the first $k$ of 16 drafted tokens accepts the shorter of the original accepted run and $k$, so one set of traces gives $\tau$ for every $k \le 16$.
\item \textbf{Throughput, not just latency.} When decoding is limited by memory bandwidth, offloading raises throughput by at most about the link-to-memory ratio, with or without speculation. We will measure where latency budgets do and do not translate into throughput.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Timeline.}
Weeks 7--8: batched verification benchmark, GH200 validation run.
Weeks 9--10: batch, buffer-count and verification-width sweeps; comparison with
the hidden-only rule. Week 11: final report and presentation.

